<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uzbek Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1282">1282</a>–<a href="/years/1341">1341</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Öz Beg Khan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> Khan was khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> from 1313 to 1341, the longest reign in its history. He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> the state religion, built mosques in the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a> and kept close ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. He controlled the Russian principalities by granting the title of grand prince, favouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> over Tver, and the Horde's trade and power reached their height in his reign.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uzbek-khan.jpg" alt="Uzbek Khan" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A. Dalorto · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Khan_Uzbek.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
